<p>The end screen is the one frame YouTube draws ON. Its elements — two video tiles and a subscribe circle — are laid over your picture at sizes it decides (a video element is 410 × 230 at 1280 × 720, so 615 × 346 here; the subscribe circle 294 across). So the frame leaves those exact windows empty, dashed in the docs, and spends the rest on the channel: the thanks, the line about Friday, the face with the subscribe pill under it where YouTube’s own will sit, a hand-written “watch this next” with an arrow at the slots, and the mark. Every line arrives on the layer system, staggered.</p>
